lesson: 87
title: Only the full ones count
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes only full groups count, and the ones left over are not enough for another group.
explain: 26 eggs in boxes of 6 fill 4 boxes, and 2 eggs are left, which is not a full box.
explain: Divide and leave out the remainder.
vars: a=name t=thing n=10..80 k=3..9
need: n % k != 0
need: n > k
answer: (n - n % k) / k
exercise: {a} packs {n} {t} into boxes of {k}. How many boxes can {a} fill?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. {a.He} makes bags of {k} {t}. How many full bags can {a.he} make?
exercise: A flower seller has {n} roses and puts {k} roses in each bunch. How many full bunches can she make?
exercise: {a} has {n} dollars. A ticket costs {k} dollars. How many tickets can {a} buy?
exercise: A cake needs {k} eggs. How many cakes can be baked with {n} eggs?
exercise: {n} children make teams of {k}. How many full teams can they make?
test: How many full packs of {k} can {a} make from {n} {t}?
test: {a} cuts a ribbon {n} cm long into pieces {k} cm long. How many whole pieces does {a.he} get?
test: One shirt needs {k} buttons. {a} has {n} buttons. For how many shirts are there enough buttons?
test: With {n} {t} and {k} {t} on every full tray, how many trays are completely full?
exercise: How many boxes of {k} can {a} fill from {n} {t}?
exercise: {a} has {n} dollars in Texas. Each comic costs {k} dollars. How many comics can {a.he} buy?
exercise: {n} {t} are put in bags of {k}. How many bags are completely full?
exercise: A recipe needs {k} apples for one pie. How many pies can {a} bake with {n} apples?
exercise: {n} children in a gym in Leeds form teams of {k}. How many complete teams are there?
exercise: {a} cuts a rope {n} m long into pieces {k} m long. How many full pieces does {a.he} get?
exercise: Each necklace needs {k} beads. {a} has {n} beads. How many necklaces can {a.he} make?
exercise: A baker in Toronto has {n} rolls and puts {k} rolls in each bag. How many full bags can the baker make?
test: Tickets for a ride in Sydney cost {k} dollars each. With {n} dollars, how many tickets can {a} afford?
test: How many whole cartons of {k} can be filled with {n} eggs?
test: {a} has {n} {t} and wants packs of exactly {k}. How many such packs can {a.he} make?
test: A toy car needs {k} wheels. {a} has {n} wheels. How many toy cars can {a.he} finish?
test: At a market in Dublin, tulips are sold in bunches of {k}. A seller has {n} tulips. How many bunches can she sell?
test: Every team needs {k} players. If {n} pupils turn up, how many teams can be complete?
exercise: {a} packs {n} {t} in boxes of {k}, how many full boxes
exercise: {a}'s got {n} bucks, a ticket's {k}. how many tickets can {a.he} get
exercise: {n} roses, {k+1} a bunch — no wait, {k}. how many full bunches
exercise: bags of {k} from {n} {t}, my mate says {(n - n % k) / k + 1}. nah the last one aint full. how many full bags
test: {n} dollars, {k} dollars a ticket. how many tickets
test: {a}'s got {n} {t}, packs em {k} to a box, nan was there too lol. how many full boxes
test: {n} eggs, cartons of {k}, {a} reckons {(n - n % k) / k + 1} full ones?? not right innit. how many full cartons
exam: A quantity of {n} {t} is packed into boxes, each holding precisely {k} {t}. Determine the number of boxes completely filled.
exam: It is given that {a} possesses {n.words} {t} and forms bags of {k.words}. Ascertain the greatest number of complete bags which may be so formed.
exam: Whereas each complete set comprises {k} {t}, state the number of complete sets obtainable from {n} {t}, any surplus being disregarded.
exam: Compute the number of whole rows of {k} which may be formed from {n} items.
exercise: Great-aunt Mary passed on and left {n} {t} in a tin. {a} makes bags of exactly {k} {t} for the family. How many full bags can {a} make?
exercise: Dad was let go from the factory, so he sells his {n} old {t} at the market in packs of exactly {k}. How many full packs can he sell?
test: {a}'s telling porkies again, said there's only a few {t} — there's {n}. how many full packs of {k} then, genius
